This study characterizes the supply of lending-based crowdfunding in Peru between 2022 and 2026 and compares it with the terms of traditional credit. A reproducible panel of #cifra("filasPanel") platform-month observations was built with data from the application programming interfaces of the Central Reserve Bank of Peru and the Superintendency of the Securities Market (SMV), and from web scraping of the SMV project portal. Each variable in the econometric tests has at least 1 000 observations or #cifra("muSeriesObs") monthly periods. Only #cifra("plataformasConFinanciamiento") of the #cifra("plataformasAutorizadas") platform operators funded projects, and one of them held #cifra("participacionLider")~% of the amount, which rose from S/~#cifra("monto2022") million in 2022 to S/~#cifra("monto2025") million in 2025. With #cifra("muSeriesObs") months, neither Engle-Granger nor Johansen finds cointegration between crowdfunding and traditional lending rates, and in the short run those rates are not jointly significant ($p #cifra("cpConWaldPRel")$). The 2025 regulatory amendment shows no positive effect on an amount that was already growing. Business projects are more likely to be funded (odds ratio of #cifra("logUnoOr")), an advantage that falls to #cifra("logTresOr") once platform and year are controlled for. Platform profitability does not differ significantly from that of other supervised intermediaries once size is controlled for ($p #cifra("empRoaPRel")$). Because the SMV does not publish the rate or term of each loan, the price of crowdfunding cannot be compared with that of traditional credit.

